{"id":357,"path":"/grammar-points/b1-b2/adjective-order/","parentId":332,"order":60,"section":"grammar-points","level":"b1-b2","depth":3,"title":"Adjective order","seo":{"title":"Adjective order - Test-English","description":"Learn the correct order of adjectives in English in this B1+ grammar lesson with clear rules, charts and exercises. Practise now!","image":"/media/uploads/B2.1_Adjective-order.png"},"modified":"2026-03-12T09:27:10","kind":"lesson","parts":["<h3>Exercise 1</h3> <h5>Choose suitable adjectives to complete the sentences below.</h5> <div data-quiz=\"287\"></div>","<h3>Exercise 2</h3> <h5>Choose the correct answer for the sentences below.</h5> <div data-quiz=\"288\"></div>","<h3>Exercise 3</h3> <h5>Chose the correct order for the following phrases.</h5> <div data-quiz=\"15\"></div>"],"explanation":"<p><img class=\"alignnone size-full wp-image-30272\" src=\"/media/uploads/Adjective-order_Grammar-chart.webp\" alt=\"Educational infographic detailing the correct order of adjectives in English grammar, labeled with the mnemonic 'OSASCOMP' representing opinion, size, age, shape, color, origin, material, and purpose, with examples for each category.\" width=\"500\" height=\"500\" loading=\"lazy\"></p> <h6></h6> <p>Native speakers often learn how to order <a href=\"/grammar-points/a1/adjectives/\">adjectives</a> intuitively through years of exposure and practice rather than through formal study. They develop a natural sense of what ‘sounds right’ without necessarily knowing the rules that govern these choices. However, for students of English as a second language, mastering the order of adjectives can be quite challenging. As you can see in the grammar chart above, there’s a handy mnemonic (OSASCOMP) that can help you remember the usual sequence. Fortunately, it’s rare that you’ll need to use many adjectives together, and when you do, the context of the sentence often guides the correct placement, making communication clear and effective even if the order varies slightly.</p> <h3>Opinion vs fact adjectives</h3> <p>Opinion adjectives express what the speaker thinks of something, e.g. nice, handsome, brilliant, etc., and fact adjectives give us information about something such as age, size, colour, material, etc.<br> <br> When we use two or more adjectives together, opinion adjectives usually&nbsp;go before fact adjectives.</p> <ul class=\"grammar-ex\"> <li><em>They live in an&nbsp;<span><strong>amazing&nbsp;modern</strong></span> attic.&nbsp;</em></li> <li><em>She’s a <span><strong>beautiful young</strong></span> woman.&nbsp;</em></li> </ul> <h3>Order of fact adjectives</h3> <p>When we use two or more fact adjectives together, they normally go in the following order:</p> <div class=\"textBoxwide\"> <span class=\"numBlue\">1</span> <span>OPINION:</span> wonderful, terrible, lovely<br> <br> <span class=\"numBlue\">2</span> <span>SIZE:</span> small, huge, tiny<br> <br> <span class=\"numBlue\">3</span> <span>AGE:</span> ancient, new, young<br> <br> <span class=\"numBlue\">4</span> <span>SHAPE:</span> round, square, oblong<br> <br> <span class=\"numBlue\">5</span> <span>COLOUR:</span> green, bright, pale<br> <br> <span class=\"numBlue\">6</span> <span>ORIGIN:</span> Victorian, American, Turkish<br> <br> <span class=\"numBlue\">7</span> <span>MATERIAL:</span> wooden, metallic, cotton<br> <br> <span class=\"numBlue\">8</span> <span>PURPOSE:</span> racing, sleeping, cooking </div> <h3>Determiners</h3> <p>Before any adjective-noun combination, we use one or more determiners, which may include <a href=\"/grammar-points/b1/an-the-no-article/\">articles</a>, <a href=\"/grammar-points/a1/possessive-adjectives/\">possessives</a>, <a href=\"/grammar-points/a1/this-that-these-those/\">demonstratives</a>, numbers or <a href=\"/grammar-points/b1/much-many-lot-little-no/\">quantifiers</a>: <strong>a, the, some, any, this, my, her, one, ten, many, few, etc.</strong></p>","quizzes":[{"id":287,"questions":[{"id":2475,"num":"1","type":"gaps","prompt":"<p>Do you like my <span data-gap=\"0\"></span>&nbsp;new shoes?</p>","gaps":[{"kind":"select","options":["leather","expensive","blue"]}]},{"id":2476,"num":"2","type":"gaps","prompt":"<p>Matt is an intelligent <span data-gap=\"0\"></span> old man.</p>","gaps":[{"kind":"select","options":["big","white","American"]}]},{"id":2477,"num":"3","type":"gaps","prompt":"<p>They use new&nbsp;<span data-gap=\"0\"></span> wooden spoons&nbsp;to eat.</p>","gaps":[{"kind":"select","options":["expensive","long","big"]}]},{"id":2478,"num":"4","type":"gaps","prompt":"<p>I've bought some red <span data-gap=\"0\"></span> cups for the party.</p>","gaps":[{"kind":"select","options":["small","plastic","cheap"]}]},{"id":2479,"num":"5","type":"gaps","prompt":"<p>You need to wash that <span data-gap=\"0\"></span> old blanket.</p>","gaps":[{"kind":"select","options":["red","wool","dirty"]}]},{"id":2480,"num":"6","type":"gaps","prompt":"<p>Have you seen my blue <span data-gap=\"0\"></span> running sweatshirt?</p>","gaps":[{"kind":"select","options":["cotton","new","long"]}]},{"id":2481,"num":"7","type":"gaps","prompt":"<p>I've bought a <span data-gap=\"0\"></span> antique Egyptian statue.</p>","gaps":[{"kind":"select","options":["small","black","marble"]}]},{"id":2482,"num":"8","type":"gaps","prompt":"<p>The victim&nbsp;is a middle-aged black <span data-gap=\"0\"></span> man.</p>","gaps":[{"kind":"select","options":["muscular","big","American"]}]},{"id":2483,"num":"9","type":"gaps","prompt":"<p>The murder weapon was a long <span data-gap=\"0\"></span> metal bar.</p>","gaps":[{"kind":"select","options":["big","blue","old"]}]},{"id":2484,"num":"10","type":"gaps","prompt":"<p>She is a <span data-gap=\"0\"></span> young woman.</p>","gaps":[{"kind":"select","options":["beautiful","Australian","slim"]}]}]},{"id":288,"questions":[{"id":2485,"num":"1","type":"radio","prompt":"<div>She has&nbsp;a crush on her _____ teacher.</div>","choices":[{"id":3794,"letter":"A.","html":"<span>young intellige